\section{Experiments}

All experiments use open-weight 7--9B parameter models evaluated with vLLM~0.9.2 on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (96GB) with binary correctness scoring.

\subsection{Single-Model Baseline}

We first establish the variance structure within a single model (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) on MMLU to isolate methodological facet contributions before introducing cross-model variation.

\paragraph{Design.} The G-study crosses six facets: 200 items $\times$ 3 numerical precisions (BF16, FP16, FP32) $\times$ 6 prompt templates $\times$ 6 random seeds $\times$ 3 temperature levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) $\times$ 4 answer orderings, yielding 259,200 binary observations. Variance components are estimated via Henderson Method~I with bootstrap confidence intervals (1,000 resamples).

\paragraph{Results.} Under binary scoring, item difficulty (\texttt{item\_id}) accounts for 58.4\% of total variance, with the residual term contributing 23.1\%. All main facet effects are negligible ($<$1\% each): precision 0.9\%, prompt template, temperature, seed, and ordering each $<$0.5\%. The interesting structure appears in item-conditional interactions: prompt$\times$item is the largest at 5.5\%, followed by seed$\times$item (4.7\%), precision$\times$item (3.3\%), temperature$\times$item (2.9\%), and ordering$\times$item (0.8\%). This pattern indicates that methodological choices do not shift performance uniformly across items; instead, specific items are differentially sensitive to specific facets---prompt template variation produces the largest item-conditional effect.

The resulting generalizability coefficient is $G_\text{item} = 0.936$, indicating that 200 items under multi-facet conditions produce highly reliable rankings. A D-study projection shows $G \geq 0.80$ is achievable with as few as 55 items in this multi-facet design.

For text-exact-match scoring (requiring verbatim string matches), the variance structure shifts dramatically: temperature dominates at 79.7\% of variance, and $G_\text{item}$ collapses to 0.007. This metric-conditional divergence motivates our exclusive use of binary correctness for the remaining analyses.

\subsection{Cross-Model Variance}

Extending to eight models (BF16 subset, 460,800 records), Table~\ref{tab:cross_model} shows that item difficulty drops from 58.4\% to 37.9\%, while model$\times$item interaction emerges at up to 36\%. The model main effect is 3--5\% (MC) and 9--11\% (FF), confirming that models differ primarily in \emph{which items} they find difficult rather than in overall ability level.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Variance decomposition for the cross-model MMLU design (8 models, BF16, 460,800 records). Components estimated via Henderson Method~I. $G_\text{item} = 0.896$.}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lr}
\toprule
Component & Var.\% \\
\midrule
\textbf{item\_id} & \textbf{37.9} \\
\underline{model$\times$item} & \underline{30.2} \\
residual & 25.0 \\
model & 2.8 \\
prompt$\times$item & 2.5 \\
model$\times$prompt & 1.6 \\
all others & $<$1.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The per-model interaction magnitudes reveal meaningful asymmetry, suggesting that instruction-tuning strategies produce model-specific item difficulty profiles.

\subsection{Format-Associated Variance Structure}
\label{sec:format_conditional}

We extend to five benchmarks (three MC: MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag; two FF: GSM8K, MATH) using exp-002 (8 models $\times$ BF16 $\times$ 2 temperatures $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 4 orderings $\times$ 200 items; MATH uses 7 models due to incomplete DeepSeek inference data). Table~\ref{tab:cross_benchmark} reveals a format-associated pattern. For all three MC benchmarks, item-related variance (item + model$\times$item DIF; e.g., MMLU: 37.9\% + 30.2\% = 68.1\%) concentrates at 68--72\%. HellaSwag is the sole MC case where model$\times$item slightly exceeds item (36.0\% vs.\ 34.3\%), though their sum remains in the MC range.

Free-form benchmarks differ markedly. On GSM8K, variance distributes across item (16.9\%), model$\times$item (14.6\%), model (10.7\%), and model$\times$prompt (8.5\%); prompt main effect is 5.4\%. MATH shows an intermediate pattern with 7 models (DeepSeek excluded): item leads at 24.0\% with model$\times$item at 17.3\%, but residual variance is substantially larger (43.9\%). Cross-benchmark LOMO analysis shows that removing any single model changes $G_\text{item}$ by less than 0.02 across all four benchmarks with complete 8-model data (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}). The 7-model MATH analysis may conservatively underestimate model-related variance, as DeepSeek exclusion shifts variance from model$\times$item to residual based on these cross-benchmark LOMO trends. The large FF residuals (40--44\%) likely reflect chain-of-thought path diversity and answer extraction variability. Since scoring granularity fundamentally reshapes variance structure (binary $G = 0.936$ vs.\ text-exact-match $G = 0.007$; \S4.1), our format-associated findings are conditional on binary scoring.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Format-associated variance structure across five benchmarks (exp-002, 8 models; MATH: 7). Variance components (\%) per benchmark; MC = multiple-choice, FF = free-form. Bold indicates the dominant non-residual component per benchmark. For MC benchmarks, ``prompt'' reports prompt$\times$item (prompt main effect $<$1\%); for FF benchmarks, it reports the prompt main effect.}
\label{tab:cross_benchmark}
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{l rrr rr}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{3}{c}{MC} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{FF} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6}
 & MMLU & ARC & Hella. & GSM & MATH \\
\midrule
\textbf{item} & \textbf{37.9} & \textbf{37.9} & 34.3 & 16.9 & 24.0 \\
mod.$\times$item & 30.2 & 33.7 & \textbf{36.0} & 14.6 & 17.3 \\
model & 2.8 & 3.1 & 5.4 & 10.7 & 8.5 \\
prompt & 2.5 & 0.4 & 2.3 & 5.4 & 1.0 \\
mod.$\times$prmpt & 1.6 & 8.0 & 0.6 & 8.5 & 2.0 \\
residual & 25.0 & 15.4 & 21.4 & 39.9 & 43.9 \\
all others & $<$1.0 & 1.5 & $<$1.0 & 4.0 & 3.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The observed variance structure is format-associated: MC benchmarks concentrate item-related variance at 68--72\%, while FF benchmarks disperse it to 31--41\%, with no overlap. Critically, even restricting to item difficulty alone (excluding model$\times$item DIF), MC benchmarks show 34--38\% versus FF 17--24\%, maintaining complete separation (unbundled benchmark-level $d = 4.84$, bootstrap 95\% CI $[2.32, 8.19]$), confirming that the format gap is not an artifact of DIF bundling. A permutation test yields $p = 0.10$ ($\binom{5}{3} = 10$ exhaustive partitions), the resolution floor---rejecting at $\alpha = 0.05$ is impossible regardless of effect magnitude. The item-level effect size is tightly bounded ($d = 1.73$; 95\% CI $[1.72, 1.83]$), but the benchmark-level CI spans zero ($[-1.7, 5.1]$, $df = 3$). We therefore present the format-associated pattern as a descriptive observation with strong item-level support rather than a confirmatory finding. This does not extend to item budgets: GSM8K requires 128 items for $G \geq 0.80$ while HellaSwag (MC, 118) exceeds both ARC (98) and MATH (101), showing that format alone does not predict evaluation cost. Figure~\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} visualizes the contrast.

One might argue the format gap is tautological: MC constrains generation noise, mechanically inflating item-related shares. Two observations argue against this. First, HellaSwag is MC yet model$\times$item (36.0\%) exceeds item (34.3\%)---pure format effects would produce uniform item dominance. Second, within MMLU's 57 subjects, difficulty dispersion correlates with item variance ($\rho = 0.806$, $p = 0.005$, $n = 10$), and \texttt{high\_school\_mathematics} (open-ended numerical items) shows item variance of just 10.4\%, below GSM8K (16.9\%). This extends across benchmarks: cross-benchmark $\text{std}(p_i)$ correlates with \texttt{item\_id} share ($\rho = 0.82$, $p = 0.089$, $n = 5$; Appendix~\ref{app:difficulty_scatter}), with MC benchmarks showing higher dispersion ($0.27$--$0.31$) than FF ($0.22$--$0.23$). Format constrains the noise floor, but item difficulty heterogeneity determines the magnitude of item-related variance within that floor.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_cross_benchmark}
\caption{Variance decomposition (Henderson Method~I) across five benchmarks and eight models (MATH: seven). Multiple-choice benchmarks share an item-dominated structure; HellaSwag is the exception where model$\times$item (36.0\%) exceeds item difficulty (34.3\%). Free-form benchmarks (GSM8K, MATH) distribute variance across item, model, and interaction components with no single dominant source.}
\label{fig:cross_benchmark}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Precision Separability}

Under binary scoring, BF16 and FP16 are functionally interchangeable: mean accuracy differs by 0.23 percentage points, and all precision$\times$methodology interactions contribute $<$0.1\% of variance \cite{yuan2025layercast}. Under text-exact-match scoring, a precision$\times$temperature interaction emerges at 1.3\%, consistent with the metric-conditional pattern in \S4.1. For standard binary evaluation at BF16/FP16 on single-GPU inference, numerical precision is a negligible variance source that need not be controlled \cite{shanmugavelu2024floating}. For non-binary metrics (e.g., BLEU, exact string match), our budgets do not apply; the text-exact-match collapse ($G = 0.007$) suggests such metrics require fundamentally larger budgets.

\subsection{Design-Dependent Reproducibility Budgets}

How many items does a given evaluation design need to achieve a target reliability? We translate variance components into item budgets via D-study projections.

Table~\ref{tab:dstudy} compares evaluation designs at the $G \geq 0.80$ threshold using single-model (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) MMLU variance components. The multi-facet design (1,296 conditions per item) achieves $G = 0.936$ at 200 items and requires only 55 items for $G \geq 0.80$. A single-condition design reaches only $G = 0.592$ at 200 items and requires 552 items---a 10.0$\times$ budget ratio stable across subset sizes (CV = 1.9\%).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{D-study reproducibility budgets under different evaluation designs (single-model, MMLU). Total evals = items $\times$ conditions.}
\label{tab:dstudy}
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{lcrrrc}
\toprule
Design & Cond. & $n_{G{\geq}.80}$ & Total & $G_{200}$ & $G_{116}$ \\
\midrule
Multi-facet & 1{,}296 & \textbf{55} & 71{,}280 & \textbf{.936} & --- \\
Single-cond. & 1 & 552 & 552 & .592 & .457 \\
IRT+multi & 1{,}296 & --- & 150{,}336 & --- & .895$^\ddagger$ \\
\midrule
IRT baseline & --- & 116 & 116 & --- & .457$^\dagger$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\vspace{2pt}
{\scriptsize $^\dagger$\,PSN-IRT 116 items \cite{zhou2026irt} under single-condition: $G = 0.457$. $^\ddagger$\,Same 116 items under multi-facet: $G = 0.895$.}
\end{table}

\citet{zhou2026irt}'s IRT-selected 116 items yield $G = 0.457$ under single-condition but $G = 0.895$ under multi-facet conditions, demonstrating that IRT item selection and G-theory protocol optimization are orthogonal and complementary. The higher total evaluation count (71,280 vs.\ 552) reflects the cost of reliable measurement. These projections have out-of-sample support: four-model subset D-study predictions match the observed eight-model coefficient within $\Delta = 0.003$ (\S\ref{sec:model_facet}). Budgets are validated within our model universe (open-weight, 7--9B, instruction-tuned); practitioners should verify applicability to their specific model population.

For practitioners seeking a minimal upgrade, 3 prompts $\times$ 3 seeds ($G_{200} = 0.766$, $n_{G \geq 0.80} \approx 140$) is the cheapest design exceeding $G = 0.80$; a full adoption gradient is in Appendix~\ref{app:intermediate_dstudy}. Cross-model D-study projections (Appendix~\ref{app:dstudy_cross}) reveal that the efficiency gap is itself format-associated: MC benchmarks show 11--13$\times$ ratios while FF benchmarks show 21--22$\times$ (bootstrap CIs confirm all exceed 10$\times$), making multi-facet designs especially critical for reasoning tasks. Figure~\ref{fig:dstudy} plots the D-study curves; the multi-facet curve saturates beyond $\sim$150 items, while the single-condition curve does not reach $G = 0.80$ within typical benchmark sizes.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_dstudy_curves}
\caption{D-study projection curves (single-model, MMLU). The multi-facet design (10.0$\times$ fewer items) reaches $G \geq 0.80$ at 55 items; the single-condition design requires 552 items. Cross-model per-benchmark projections are in Appendix~\ref{app:dstudy_cross}. Shaded band: 95\% bootstrap CIs.}
\label{fig:dstudy}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Preliminary Scale Validation}

A within-family analysis (Qwen2.5 3B and 7B on MMLU, same design) confirms item dominance is scale-robust: \texttt{item\_id} accounts for 75.3\% (3B) and 73.4\% (7B), with $G = 0.963$ and $0.956$ respectively. Minor scale-dependent prompt sensitivity appears (prompt$\times$item: 12.2\% at 7B vs.\ 8.8\% at 3B). Extension to 70B+ models remains for future work.

\subsection{Robustness Checks}

Three independent 200-item MMLU samples yield item difficulty variance shares of 73.2\%, 72.6\%, and 69.0\% (CV = 3.1\%), confirming sampling stability. Seven additional analyses (GLMM, accuracy-range, estimator comparison, CI method, facet specification, LOMO, null calibration) in Appendix~\ref{app:robustness} all confirm stability.

\appendix
\section{Additional Robustness Analyses}
\label{app:robustness}

We report eight robustness analyses that verify the stability of the variance decomposition under alternative analytical choices.

\paragraph{GLMM complement.} A generalized linear mixed model (GLMM) with marginal logit link produces variance component rankings that perfectly preserve all seven grouped component orderings ($\rho = 1.0$) relative to Henderson Method~I \cite{jiang2018bayesian, tuerlinckx2006glmm}. This confirms that the linear variance decomposition is robust to the binary outcome distribution.

\paragraph{Accuracy-range stability.} The \texttt{item\_id} variance share remains stable across subsets with mean accuracy from 0.62 to 0.72 ($r = -0.048$). This rules out distortion from binary floor or ceiling effects at observed accuracy levels.

\paragraph{Estimator robustness.} We compare Henderson Method~I with restricted maximum likelihood (REML-EM, 500 iterations, practical convergence $\Delta < 0.003$ percentage points). All variance components differ by $<$0.5 percentage points between estimators. The largest discrepancy occurs on the residual component (0.47pp); all substantive components (item, model$\times$item, model) differ by $<$0.3pp. Henderson Method~I is preferred for our balanced designs because it produces unbiased estimates without distributional assumptions \cite[Ch.~12]{brennan2001generalizability}.

\paragraph{CI method comparison.} Bootstrap (1,000 item-level resamples with replacement) and delete-1 jackknife confidence intervals agree closely: width ratios range from 0.96 to 1.04 across all variance components. The agreement indicates that the dependence structure introduced by item-level bootstrap resampling (which creates $\sim$127 unique items per 200-item resample) does not materially distort interval estimates. Stratified resampling by subject (10 subjects $\times$ 20 items) produces CIs within 4\% of simple item-level bootstrap, confirming that the within-subject dependence structure does not materially affect interval estimates.

\paragraph{Facet specification sensitivity.} Treating temperature and ordering as fixed (rather than random) facets changes $G_\text{item}$ by only 0.37 percentage points (all-random: 0.820 vs.\ fixed temperature+ordering: 0.824). The small difference reflects the negligible temperature$\times$item (0.65\%) and ordering$\times$item (0.54\%) variance shares. The random-facet assumption is therefore robust to facet classification.

\paragraph{Leave-one-model-out (LOMO).} All eight seven-model subsets preserve the item + model$\times$item dominance pattern: item\_id ranges from 36\% to 41\%, and $G_\text{item}$ ranges from 0.877 to 0.889. No single model disproportionately drives the observed variance structure. The consistency of component rankings across all subsets supports the quasi-random model facet assumption (\S\ref{sec:model_facet}). Table~\ref{tab:lomo_cross} extends the LOMO analysis to all four benchmarks with complete 8-model data, confirming that $|\Delta G_\text{item}| < 0.02$ uniformly.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cross-benchmark DeepSeek LOMO: effect of removing DeepSeek from each benchmark with complete 8-model data.}
\label{tab:lomo_cross}
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{llrr}
\toprule
Benchmark & Format & $\Delta G_\text{item}$ & $\Delta$item\% \\
\midrule
ARC & MC & $+$0.002 & $+$3.5 \\
HellaSwag & MC & $-$0.006 & $+$2.6 \\
GSM8K & FF & $-$0.009 & $+$0.4 \\
MMLU & MC & $-$0.018 & $+$1.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Null calibration.} We generate 100 Bernoulli-distributed datasets with item difficulties matched to the empirical distribution but no systematic facet effects. The observed-to-null variance ratio is 1.0008, confirming that Henderson Method~I does not introduce systematic bias when applied to binary outcomes. The null simulation also verifies that the item variance share (58.4\%) is not an artifact of the Bernoulli $p(1{-}p)$ ceiling: the null produces item variance of 0.08\%, three orders of magnitude below the observed value. Across all 100 replications, the same three components (item, model$\times$item, residual) emerge as the top three (set consistency 100\%), with Kendall's $\tau = 0.81$ between binary and continuous rankings.

\paragraph{Reduced crossing validation.} To verify that the reduced crossing in exp-002 (BF16 only $\times$ 2 temperatures) does not introduce confounding, we extracted the corresponding subset from the fully crossed exp-001 design. The generalizability coefficient differs by only 0.0008 ($G = 0.9361$ vs.\ $0.9369$), and all non-precision variance components differ by less than 2 percentage points. The 10pp increase in item variance share (58.4\% $\to$ 68.5\%) is fully explained by the removal of precision-related components ($\sim$4.5\% total), which redistributes across remaining sources. This confirms that the exp-002 design preserves the variance structure of the full crossing.

\section{Cross-Model D-Study Projections}
\label{app:dstudy_cross}

Table~\ref{tab:dstudy_cross} extends the single-model D-study (Table~\ref{tab:dstudy} in main text) to per-benchmark cross-model projections with bootstrap confidence intervals (1,000 item-level resamples, 8 models, 2,304 conditions per item). The efficiency ratio is format-associated: MC benchmarks show 11--13$\times$ (mean 12.0$\times$ [11.6, 12.5]), while FF benchmarks show 21--22$\times$ (mean 21.5$\times$ [20.4, 22.8]).

